=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that all Location C machines (PC11–PC14, on the 192.168.200.0/24 subnet behind router4) are completely unreachable from any other site and cannot even reach their own gateway — is a false premise. Direct probing of the live lab shows the network is operating correctly in all measurable respects.

Router4 (the Location C gateway at 192.168.200.1) has its LAN-facing interface eth0 UP, IP forwarding enabled, no iptables rules, and a full RIP-derived routing table including routes to all known subnets. Pings from every tested Location C machine (PC11, PC12, PC13, PC14) to the gateway succeed with zero packet loss. End-to-end reachability to Location A (172.16.10.x and 172.16.20.x, served via router5 and router0) is confirmed in both directions: PC11→PC4, PC12→PC4, PC14→PC4, and PC4→PC11 all succeed. Router0 carries a RIP-learned route to 192.168.200.0/24, confirming symmetric routing topology-wide.

The only observed "failure" is PC11→PC15 (172.18.51.15, Location B), which is caused by intentional iptables DROP rules on router6's WAN-facing eth2 interface — a documented security policy that blocks all inbound IP traffic from outside Location B except a narrow TCP/80 permit for a specific source. This is design, not a fault.

No corrective action is needed. The ticket likely stems from a transient user-perceived issue (possibly a brief routing convergence delay after lab startup) or a misunderstanding about which destinations are reachable by policy. The Location C network is healthy.
=== END ===